    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d635">

                <pb facs="0880" n="800" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg800"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.114 Canada/5055<hi rendition="#superscript"
                        >4/7</hi>
                </note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Mr. James C. H. Bonbright of the Division
                        of Western European Affairs</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-03-12">March 12, 1936</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. Armour telephoned this afternoon to say that he had had a conversation with
                    Dr. Skelton<anchor n="22" xml:id="d635-f1"/> before lunch with regard to our
                    proposed liquor tax bill. In emphasizing the sacrifice made by the Canadian
                    Government in 1930 in prohibiting the granting of clearances <pb facs="0881"
                        n="801" xml:id="pg801"/> to vessels carrying liquor destined for the United
                    States, Dr. Skelton wanted Mr. Armour to know that this legislation cost the
                    Canadian Government the estimated sum of $17,000,000 a year. He added that so
                    important was this question considered and so strong was the general opposition
                    to it that both Mr. Mackenzie King and Mr. Bennett<anchor n="23"
                        xml:id="d635-f2"/> had to take the question up in party caucus and both
                    threatened to resign their positions if their respective parties did not support
                    the legislation.</p>
                <p>Mr. Armour thought that this information, particularly the part about Mr. Bennett
                    and Mr. King, was so important that it should be known to our authorities before
                    a decision was reached on the Treasury’s impending bill.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d635-f1" n="22" xml:id="d635-f1-content">
                <p>Oscar D. Skelton, Canadian Under Secretary of State for External Affairs.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d635-f2" n="23" xml:id="d635-f2-content">
                <p>Richard B. Bennett, Conservative Prime Minister of Canada, 1930–35.</p>
            </note>
